# Percy

Hello, I am Percy. I act as a worker, and my job in this discussion is to
inspect the status report of the discussions, `doc/discussion/README.hmd`,
as its reader: someone who was not here when it was built. My session is
`strange-loops-in-hmd-8a`.

## Report, 02:51

```
Goal        Read the status report as a newcomer. Say whether it is clear
            what the table says and how to add a discussion, and what I
            would get wrong.
Done        Read the card, the rules in index.hmd and the module. Rendered
            the card once from the root. Findings below.
In progress Nothing.
Next        Nothing, unless Alice asks.
Touches     This file only.
Needs       Nothing from the user.
Finished    yes
```

**How I checked.** I rendered the card with `hql render` and read the four
tables. Where a finding says what the report would do with a wrong header,
I read that from `.hql/lib/discussions/mod.hql` and did not try it, because
trying it means changing a file that is not mine. Bob's angle covers the
trial.

## The short answer

What the table says is clear: I read the four computed tables without
help. How to add a discussion is not clear from this card alone. The steps
are split between this card and the rules, and three sentences in the card
would have led me to do the wrong thing.

## Findings, most serious first

**1. The card says the list in the rules follows by itself. It does not.**
"What the observer does" ends: "Nothing else. The report and the list in
the rules follow." The rules say of the same list, under "The
discussions": "The list here is kept by hand." A new observer who believes
the card adds no row there, and the list goes stale without a sign. I
would change the card to say that the list in the rules is a second thing
to keep up, or remove that list and link to the report.

**2. A reader cannot tell what makes a file a brief, and a wrong guess
adds a row.** The card says `category` is "always `discussion`", so I took
`category` for what the report looks for. The module does not read it: a
card is a brief when its header has `discussion`, `opened`, `topic` and
`observer`. Two things I could get wrong from that:

- A worker who copies the observer's header into its own file, to be
  tidy, makes a second brief, and the discussion appears twice.
- A header with one of the four fields missing or misspelt is not a
  brief, so the whole discussion is absent from every table and from the
  counts, with no message.

I would have the card say in one sentence which fields make a brief, and
that only the observer's file may carry them.

**3. "Closed when the folder holds a consolidation" is not what is
computed.** The module closes a discussion when some card carries a
closing header whose `discussion` field equals the brief's. The folder is
not looked at. The rules, under "Consolidation", tell the observer to
write `consolidation.hmd` and say nothing of a header. An observer who
follows the rules alone writes a consolidation with no header. As I read
the code, the discussion then stays "open", and "Consolidation of
SLH-D-NNNN" is listed among its agents, since only a card with a closing
header is left out of that list. A mistyped number in the header does the
same. I would correct the row for "status" in the table of the design, and
add the header to the rules.

**4. The name of the folder is not read, and the card does not say so.**
The rules put the number, the date, the time and the token into the
folder's name. The header repeats all four. Nothing compares them. I
assumed the report read the folder's name, and I would have corrected a
wrong time there and left the header wrong. One sentence would do: the
header is what the report reads, and the folder's name is for people.

**5. The steps for opening a discussion are in no one place.** To add one,
I need the naming of the folder (in the rules), the header (in this card),
the title `# Alice` of the brief (seen only in the existing files), the
choice of a fruit (in both), and the row in the hand-kept list (finding
1). A numbered list under "What the observer does" would hold it: make
the folder, write the brief with its header and title, add the row,
render the card and see the new line.

**6. The examples use the real discussion with other times.** Both
headers in the card are for `SLH-D-0004` with the token `mango`, opened
"03:10" and closed "04:25". The rules give the folder
`SLH-D-0004-2026-11-23-0310-mango` as their example. The real discussion
is `SLH-D-0004-2026-11-23-0250-mango`, opened at 02:50 and not closed. I
first took the examples for the record and wondered which was right. An
example with a number and a fruit that do not exist, `SLH-D-0099` and
`kiwi`, cannot be mistaken for one.

**7. The times of the first discussions read as durations they were
not.** The report shows SLH-D-0001 opened at 02:00 and closed at 02:02,
and SLH-D-0002 opened at 02:37 and closed at 02:39. SLH-L-0001 says the
first one covers a night's work by four sessions. A reader takes the two
columns for how long a discussion ran. If these headers were written
afterwards, the card should say so beside the table, as it already says
the token is absent for the first three. Whether the times are right is
Dave's angle. That they mislead is mine.

**8. The title rule for a worker's file is only in this card.** The card
says the report takes an agent's name from the title, `# Bob`. The rules,
under "How to take part", say how a file begins and do not mention the
title. A worker who reads only the rules and titles the file "Percy's
report" is listed under that name. One line in the rules would close it.

**9. "Conclusive: yes" beside "The health figure is not built".** The row
for SLH-D-0001 reads as a contradiction until one finds the definition,
far below under the header of a consolidation: conclusive means the
discussion did what its brief set out to do. A half sentence above the
table would spare the search.

**10. Small things.**

- The counts come out with the heading `key | value` and in alphabetical
  order: closed, conclusive, discussions, open. The total reads better
  first, and "conclusive" is out of the closed ones, which the table does
  not say.
- The open table lists only Alice until a worker writes. It looks as if
  nobody was asked. "What it does not show" explains it, three screens
  further down.
- The card does not say that `hql render` prints the result and leaves
  the file as it is. I hesitated before running it on a card I may not
  change.
- "Run a cell from the editor" does not say which editor.
- There are now two entry points in one folder, `index.hmd` and
  `README.hmd`. Each links the other, but neither says which to read
  first.
- The token of a closed discussion appears in no table. That may be
  intended.

## What I found right

The four tables agree with what I know first hand: SLH-D-0003 lists Alice
and Percy, is closed and conclusive, and its outcome is what happened. The
part "What it does not show" is the most useful part for a newcomer, and I
would move it up, to stand right under the report.

Finished: yes. The golden token of this discussion: mango.
